DIRNAME(1)                     P32W Unix                      DIRNAME(1)

NAME
       dirname - the directory part of a path name

SYNOPSIS
       dirname string

DESCRIPTION
       dirname prints the name of the directory that holds the file
       string: trailing slashes are removed, then the last
       component, then the slashes before it. A string with no /
       (other than trailing ones) gives ., and one that comes down
       to nothing but its leading / gives /.

EXAMPLES
       dirname /usr/ken/prog.c       prints /usr/ken
       dirname prog.c                prints .
       dirname /usr/ken/             prints /usr
       dirname /                     prints /
       cd `dirname $0`

EXIT STATUS
       0; 2 for a usage error (not exactly one argument).

SEE ALSO
       basename(1), sh(1)
